WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.015
Large context can fail in ways that are easy to miss.

00:00:04.015 --> 00:00:06.863
One commonly discussed issue is the lost-in-the-middle effect,

00:00:06.863 --> 00:00:11.846
where important information buried deep inside a long context may be underused or missed.

00:00:11.846 --> 00:00:15.050
The exact behavior depends on the model and task,

00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:18.965
but the practical lesson is clear: placement and emphasis still matter.

00:00:18.965 --> 00:00:22.169
Do not assume that every token receives equal attention.

00:00:22.169 --> 00:00:26.796
Irrelevant context can reduce reliability by distracting the model or encouraging shallow synthesis.

00:00:26.796 --> 00:00:30.356
Stale context can conflict with current facts, decisions, or requirements.

00:00:30.356 --> 00:00:34.271
Conflicting instructions can cause the model to follow the wrong priority.

00:00:34.271 --> 00:00:38.899
If a prompt contains old meeting notes, current policy, examples, and user preferences,

00:00:38.899 --> 00:00:42.458
the model needs help understanding which material controls the answer.

00:00:42.458 --> 00:00:47.797
Prompt injection and context poisoning are serious concerns when retrieved or user-supplied material is included.

00:00:47.797 --> 00:00:52.781
A document may contain text that tries to redirect the model, reveal hidden instructions,

00:00:52.781 --> 00:00:55.628
ignore the user's task, or change output requirements.

00:00:55.628 --> 00:00:59.900
Large context increases the amount of untrusted text that may be present.

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:04.171
Workflows should separate instructions from data and treat retrieved text as evidence,

00:01:04.171 --> 00:01:04.883
not authority.

00:01:04.883 --> 00:01:09.155
Hallucinated synthesis can happen when the model blends sources into an answer

00:01:09.155 --> 00:01:12.002
that sounds coherent but is not fully supported.

00:01:12.002 --> 00:01:14.850
Overconfident summaries may hide uncertainty or omit exceptions.

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:19.121
Missed details can be especially costly when the user assumes a large

00:01:19.121 --> 00:01:21.613
context window means the model checked everything.

00:01:21.613 --> 00:01:26.240
More context can improve performance, but it can also reduce reliability if selection,

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:28.020
structure, and review are weak.
